steps.body:
The median is the middle value once the numbers are sorted. When numbers keep arriving, sorting again after each one costs O(n log n) every time. Two heaps keep the middle ready instead:

- `low`, a `MaxPriorityQueue`, holds the smaller half, so `low.front()` is the biggest small number.
- `high`, a `MinPriorityQueue`, holds the larger half, so `high.front()` is the smallest big number.

To add `x`, enqueue it into `low`, move `low`'s top over to `high`, and if `high` is now bigger, move its top back:

```javascript harness
const { MinPriorityQueue, MaxPriorityQueue } = require("@datastructures-js/priority-queue");
const low = new MaxPriorityQueue();
const high = new MinPriorityQueue();
for (const x of [5, 15, 1]) {
  {{code}}
}
console.log(low.front(), high.front());
```

```javascript
low.enqueue(x);
high.enqueue(low.dequeue()); // the biggest small number moves up
if (high.size() > low.size()) {
  low.enqueue(high.dequeue()); // low keeps the extra one
}
```

Passing every number through `low` keeps all of `low` at or below all of `high`. The sizes never differ by more than one, and `low` holds the extra. So with an odd count the median is `low.front()`, and with an even count it's the average of the two tops, `(low.front() + high.front()) / 2`.

Each new number costs a few heap operations on heaps of about n / 2 items, so O(log n), and reading the median is O(1). The two heaps hold every number, O(n) space.

**The gotcha:** `front()` on an empty heap returns `null`, and `null + 5` is 5. Check `isEmpty()` before you ask for a median.

example.code:
const { MinPriorityQueue, MaxPriorityQueue } = require("@datastructures-js/priority-queue");
const input = require("fs").readFileSync(0, "utf8");

const low = new MaxPriorityQueue(); // the smaller half
const high = new MinPriorityQueue(); // the larger half
for (const line of input.trim().split("\n")) {
  const [name, minutes] = line.trim().split(/\s+/);
  low.enqueue(Number(minutes));
  high.enqueue(low.dequeue());
  if (high.size() > low.size()) {
    low.enqueue(high.dequeue());
  }
  const median = low.size() > high.size() ? low.front() : (low.front() + high.front()) / 2;
  console.log(`${name}: median ${median.toFixed(1)}`);
}

