steps.body:
To merge `k` sorted lists into one, you keep taking the smallest value still at the front of any list. Checking every front costs O(k) per value. A heap holds one front per list instead and hands over the smallest in O(log k).

Each entry is a `[value, list, index]` array: the value, which list it came from, and where it sits in that list. Order the heap by value, and since a heap isn't stable, break ties by list number. After you dequeue an entry, enqueue the next value from the same list, if it has one:

```javascript
const { PriorityQueue } = require("@datastructures-js/priority-queue");
const lists = [[1, 4, 7], [2, 5], [], [0, 6, 9]];
const heap = new PriorityQueue((a, b) => a[0] - b[0] || a[1] - b[1]);
lists.forEach((list, i) => {
  if (list.length > 0) {
    heap.enqueue([list[0], i, 0]);
  }
});
const merged = [];
while (!heap.isEmpty()) {
  const [value, i, j] = heap.dequeue();
  merged.push(value);
  if (j + 1 < lists[i].length) {
    heap.enqueue([lists[i][j + 1], i, j + 1]);
  }
}
console.log(merged.join(" ")); // 0 1 2 4 5 6 7 9
```

An empty list has no front, so it never gets an entry. The heap never holds more than `k` entries, one per list, so merging `N` values in all costs O(N log k) time and O(k) extra space, where joining everything and sorting costs O(N log N). The heap also lets you stop early, after the first few values.

